-- ============================================================================
-- B5 FOLLOW-ON W4a (typed-case panel modes): torquedrag.
--
-- Plan of record: docs/graded-field-audit/FOLLOW-ON-PROGRAMME.md section 1
-- route (b), and docs/graded-field-audit/w4a/torquedrag.json. Owner approved D1
-- to D7 as recommended, 2026-09-21 (D2: the panel guard forbids a DEFAULT
-- state that lands on a graded answer; typing the case is the work).
--
-- BEGINNER
--   The slant and horizontal hookloads need the capstone's 1500 kg/m3 mud, which
--   no explorer took, and printed in kN to 3 dp against tol 0.5 N. W4a gives all
--   three explorers a mud density box (blank means the lessons' 1440) and prints
--   hookloads in N to 2 dp. The brief now names the box. Prompt only.
--   PROMPT was: Six numbers from three wells, at a mud density of 1500 kg/m3.
--   PROMPT now: Six numbers from three wells, at a mud density of 1500 kg/m3.
--   Type 1500 into the string explorer's mud density box and its views run this
--   case, with hookloads in newtons to two decimals.
--
-- INTERMEDIATE
--   Torques, side force, minimum tension and the calibrated friction factor all
--   need the 1500 kg/m3 mud. W4a adds the mud box, prints torques in N.m to 3 dp
--   and tensions in N to 2 dp, and passes the mud to the friction calibration.
--   The brief now says where each field is read. Prompt only.
--   PROMPT was: (6) the OPEN-HOLE friction factor that makes the build and hold
--   well's pick-up hookload exactly 1100000 N at that mud density, holding the
--   cased-hole factor at 0.25.
--   PROMPT now: (6) the OPEN-HOLE friction factor that makes the build and hold
--   well's pick-up hookload exactly 1100000 N at that mud density, holding the
--   cased-hole factor at 0.25. Type 1500 into the mud density box of the string
--   and friction explorers: fields 1 to 5 are rows of the string explorer's
--   Every operation view, and field 6 is the friction explorer's Back it out of
--   a trip view.
--
-- ADVANCED
--   The torsion utilization and the wear need the 1500 kg/m3 mud, and the wear
--   needs the two-entry schedule, where the wear view took one entry at 120 rpm.
--   W4a adds the mud box, a fraction tile for the torsion utilization at 6 dp,
--   and three schedule rows (the lessons' 50 h at 120 rpm and two blank). The
--   brief now names the views. Prompt only.
--   PROMPT was: (6) the worst WALL LOSS as a percentage from the same run.
--   PROMPT now: (6) the worst WALL LOSS as a percentage from the same run. Type
--   1500 into the buckling and wear explorer's mud density box: field 3 is its
--   What the pipe has left view, and fields 5 and 6 are its casing wear view
--   with the two schedule entries typed into its rows.
--
-- WHAT DOES NOT MOVE. `fields` (every key, label, unit, expected and
-- tolerance), title, dataset, status and the row. The guard requires `fields`
-- to equal its live value exactly, before and after.
--
-- ATTEMPTS. Prompt copy only: no graded key or tolerance moves, so no attempt
-- can be graded differently and there is no attempts guard.
--
-- GUARDS. Each row must hold EITHER its live prompt (by md5) and fields (exact
-- jsonb) as the W1-state scratch replay has them (it is rewritten), OR its W4a
-- form (left alone). Anything else raises and the whole file rolls back. A
-- file that will write first checks that W1 is applied and refuses without it.
-- Generated by docs/graded-field-audit/w4a_capstones.py. SAFE TO RE-RUN: a
-- second run writes nothing.
-- ============================================================================

do $$
declare
